// `assumes` grades by closure, not by stamp alone. A declaration's EFFECTIVE
// grade is the weakest claim anywhere beneath it (Lean's `#print axioms`
// showing [sorryAx] under a theorem that uses `sorry`). The authored stamp is
// never overridden, but the effective grade tells the truth about what the
// claim rests on:
//
//   claimed  [proven] bought-with-debt      <- authored stamp
//   ↓ effective: unclaimed                 <- the closure contains no claim
//
// This chains: claimed calls bought (unclaimed), which calls stream
// (aspirational — honest admitted debt). The proven claim's effective grade
// must NOT be proven; the aspirational's own effective is aspirational
// (admitted debt shows as itself), and the unclaimed middleman is the silence
// the audit exists to surface.
//
//   koruc <file> assumes claimed

~import std/rules

~[
- aspirational someday-streams
  Honest admitted debt: this slurps, not built yet.
]tor stream { n: i64 } -> i64

~tor bought { n: i64 }
~bought = stream(n: 1)

~[
- proven bought-with-debt
  Claims proven, but its closure rests on the aspirational above.
]tor claimed { n: i64 }
~claimed = bought(n: 2)